<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- MATH 14 - HW7, online version: the same problems as the written       -->
<!-- Assignment 7, rebuilt as auto-graded exercises.  Same "fillin-basic"     -->
<!-- pattern as hw1-online.ptx: a <var/> per blank in the statement, matched  -->
<!-- in order by a <var> in <setup>, whose first <condition> is the correct   -->
<!-- answer.  Every blank is graded on its own, so partial credit survives.   -->
<section xml:id="sec-exercises-hw7-online" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Assignment 7</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Sections: <xref ref="section-triple-integrals-spherical" text="title"/> and <xref ref="section-jacobian-substitution" text="title"/>. Work each problem, then type your answers into the boxes and have them checked here.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every answer box is graded separately, so a box you fill in correctly
      earns its credit even if another box in the same problem is wrong.
      Press <q>Check Me</q> to submit; you may check as often as you like.
    </p>
    <p>
      <term>How to type your answers.</term> Numbers: a decimal number and
      nothing else<mdash/>no units, no <m>=</m> sign, no <m>\pi</m>; give at
      least three decimals (for instance <c>18.850</c>, not <c>6pi</c>).
      Answers are accepted within a small tolerance. Words: type just the
      word asked for, in lower case.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-hw7-online">

  <exercise xml:id="ex-hw7o-hemisphere">
    <title>A Hemisphere in Spherical Coordinates</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Evaluate
      </p>
      <me>
        \int_{0}^{6}\!\!\int_{-\sqrt{36-x^2}}^{\sqrt{36-x^2}}\!\!
        \int_{-\sqrt{36-x^2-z^2}}^{\sqrt{36-x^2-z^2}}
        \frac{dy\,dz\,dx}{\left(x^2+y^2+z^2\right)^{1/2}} .
      </me>
      <p>
        The solid of integration is half of a ball; its radius is
        <var width="6"/>, and the value of the integral is
        <var width="12"/>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <setup>
      <var>
        <condition number="6" tolerance="0.01">
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Not quite. Type a decimal number and nothing else, to at
              least three decimal places.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
      <var>
        <condition number="113.097" tolerance="0.05">
          <feedback><p>Correct: thirty-six pi.</p></feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition number="226.195" tolerance="0.1">
          <feedback><p>That is the whole ball; the limits give only the half with x at least zero.</p></feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
    </setup>
    <solution component="hw7-solutions">
      <p>
        The bounds describe the hemisphere <m>\rho \le 6</m>,
        <m>x \ge 0</m>. Since <m>dV = \rho^2\sin\phi\,
        d\rho\,d\phi\,d\theta</m> by <xref ref="eq-dv-spherical"/> and
        the integrand is
        <m>1/\rho</m>,
      </p>
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \int_{-\pi/2}^{\pi/2}\!\!\int_0^{\pi}\!\!\int_0^6
          \rho \sin\phi \,d\rho\,d\phi\,d\theta
          \amp = \pi \cdot \frac{6^2}{2}\cdot 2
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp = 36\pi \approx 113.097 .
        </mrow>
      </md>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-hw7o-ellipse">
    <title>The Area of a Slanted Ellipse</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        With <m>s = x+4y</m>, <m>t = y</m>, the Jacobian
        <m>\left|\dfrac{\partial(x,y)}{\partial(s,t)}\right|</m>
        equals <var width="6"/>, the ellipse becomes the unit disk in the
        <m>st</m>-plane, and its area is <var width="10"/>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <setup>
      <var>
        <condition number="1" tolerance="0.01">
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </condition>
         <condition string=".*">
     <feedback>
       <p>
         Not quite. Type a decimal number and nothing else, to at least
              three decimal places.
       </p>
     </feedback>
   </condition>
 </var>
      <var>
        <condition number="3.142" tolerance="0.01">
          <feedback><p>Correct: pi.</p></feedback>
        </condition>
              <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Not quite. Type a decimal number and nothing else, to at least
              three decimal places.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
    </setup>
    <solution component="hw7-solutions">
      <p>
        Inverting gives <m>x = s-4t</m>, <m>y = t</m>, so the Jacobian
        determinant is <m>1</m> and <m>dx\,dy = ds\,dt</m>. Substituting,
        <m>x^2+8xy+17y^2 = (s-4t)^2 + 8(s-4t)t + 17t^2 = s^2+t^2</m>, so
        the region is the unit disk and
      </p>
      <me>
        \text{Area} = \iint_R dx\,dy = \iint_T ds\,dt = \pi .
      </me>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-hw7o-scaling">
    <title>Scaling a Double Integral</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose <m>\iint_D f\,dA = 3</m>, where <m>D</m> is the disk
        <m>x^2+y^2 \le 16</m>. Let <m>E</m> be the disk
        <m>x^2+y^2 \le 64</m> and
        <m>g(x,y) = 3f\left(\frac{x}{2},\frac{y}{2}\right)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The change of variables carrying <m>E</m> onto <m>D</m> multiplies
        areas by <var width="6"/>, and
        <m>\iint_E g\,dA = </m> <var width="8"/>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <setup>
      <var>
        <condition number="4" tolerance="0.01">
          <feedback><p>Correct: dx dy = 4 du dv.</p></feedback>
        </condition>
         <condition string=".*">
     <feedback>
       <p>
         Not quite. Type a decimal number and nothing else, to at least
              three decimal places.
       </p>
     </feedback>
   </condition>
 </var>
      <var>
        <condition number="36" tolerance="0.05">
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition number="9" tolerance="0.05">
          <feedback><p>You applied the factor of three but not the area factor of four.</p></feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
    </setup>
    <solution component="hw7-solutions">
      <p>
        With <m>x = 2u</m>, <m>y = 2v</m> the Jacobian is <m>4</m>, and
        <m>E</m> becomes <m>u^2+v^2 \le 16</m>, which is <m>D</m>. Hence
      </p>
      <me>
        \iint_E g\,dA = 3\iint_D f(u,v)\,(4)\,du\,dv
        = 12 \iint_D f\,dA = 12(3) = 36 .
      </me>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  </exercises>
</section>
